Date: Sat, 12 Jun 1993 14:00:15 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: SPACE/FREE: Governments in Space

Ryan Alan Porter:
> Robert A Heinlein's novel 'The Moon is a Harsh Mistress'

My all-time favorite book!

> I am less certain that this would be as workable from anywhere else in the
> solar system, but it certainly has its promise from near lunar orbit. 

>From the military standpoint, Bombarding Earth from Earth-crossing asteroids
has one huge penalty (response time), but it also has some 
overwhelming advantages:

* The delta-v needed to launch from the Moon is nearly 3 km/s;
from earth-crossing asteroids or comets intercept can be obtained
for less than 0.5 km/s in many cases.  Furthermore, with native
materials propellant equipment mass is more a function of power than 
of energy, and here the continuous low thrust rockets or magsails
have orders of magnitude advantage over lunar rockets or mass drivers.

* Materials on planetoids (unoxidized metals, volatiles) can
be processed into the desired products (warheads, propellants)
with much less equipment than the silicates and oxidized metals
on the Moon.

* A mass driver and its powerplant capable of launching on the
order of 1 ton payloads would weigh on the order of 100,000's
of tons.  If Earth can afford to launch that, it can also afford
to launch 10,000's of tons of nuclear warheads, with hundreds
of gigatons of explosive power.  The Moon lacks the necessary 
abundant volatiles to supply the industrial processes to manufacture 
such large and complex equipment.  It will be dependent on either 
Earth or the Jupiter-family comets in such matters.  

* We can get over 10 km/s in free intercept velocity with
the planetoids, to add the 11 km/s we get from Earth escape velocity 
in both cases.  This increases energy at the target by nearly
a factor of 4.  Given the impulse delta-v advantage of a factor 
of 6, energy at target/energy expended ratio is 144 times higher
for earth-crossing planetoids.

* The warheads can be made stealthy.  For example, an array of 100 steel
javelins, each 0.6m diameter by 10m long, lined end-to-end and
pointed at Earthside detectors to minimize cross-section.
With a mass driver or lunar rocket, you are detected as soon as
you launch, giving over a day's warning, but an object with a 
well-painted 1 m^2 cross-section approaching Earth at 10 km/s 
might not be detected until it's too late.   (Our current
capability is detect dark carbonaceous asteroids is limited
to about 10,000 m^2 cross-section at the distance of the Moon).

In both cases, the system must be set up years ahead of time.
In the asteroid case it will be rather difficult to set
up a weapons system with short response time; matching the
Moon's response time of 2-3 days may be quite expensive.
Our bombarding constellation might consist of hundreds of
javelin bundles in a wide variety of Earth-crossing orbits,
timed so that during any month there is a set of javelins that
can be deflected to intercept Earth with less than 0.5 km/s
delta-v.  Meteor shower engineering. :-)  A quite interesting 
constellation design problem.

In Heinlein's story, the Moon already had a multi-ton mass
driver because it was exporting grain to Earth, a scenario that 
relied on abundant volatiles on the Moon and food shortages 
on Earth.  It turns out we have vast room for productivity improvements 
in Earthside agriculture and abundant volatiles don't exist on the 
Moon.  It's easy for Earth to detect and prevent the construction
of a weapons-capable lunar mass driver; not so for more modest weapons 
built out in the IR noise of the Belt.  (Most Earth-crossing asteroids 
spend most of their time in a part of the sky that makes them practically
indistinguishable from Belt asteroids).  

Back to a previous point on this thread: I'm not convinced the
offense will have an advantage over a prepared asteroid-colony
opponent.  Both energy and steel are ridiculously cheap out
there.  If we can afford travel out there and build space colonies in
first place, we can afford to build them right.  We can build
dozens of "Earth-atmosphere equivalents" of protection against
radiation, meteors, fascist nukes, etc. just by adding more
layers of native nickel-rich iron, which can be foamed to give 
km-thick protection.  Protection is largely a function of mass and 
materials strength, but the km-scale gives psychological protection, 
not to mention generating millions of bizarre, interesting nooks 
and crannies for settlers to explore.  It may be the case that
many early colonists will be foolish and idealistic, building
space colonies along the "it should be fragile" Paolo Soleri 
model.  I will be picky about the colony I choose.

I have the same problem with Earthside "Oceania" concepts.
They don't design or budget enough for security, leaving them
open attacks as trivial as canoe raids from the King of Tonga.

Sorry for carrying on so long, but I'm addicted to this
subject. :-)

Nick Szabo			szabo@techbook.com	

