 As we approach the millennium it is clear to many of us
that the old religious and political systems only contribute
to bigotry, oppression and war among peoples of different
races, religions, ethnicities and ideologies.  Similarly, the
old systems worldwide condone male personal and political
violence and the subjugation of women.    
     Many of us have investigated new forms of conscious-ness,
new world views or paradigms, and new lifestyles with various
degrees of success and satisfaction.  Yet we despair at
offering a vision that can end the hate and violence and bring
us tolerance, equality and peace.     
     However, we need not despair because a coherent new world
view or vision is evolving.  While it is not yet thoroughly
systematized, this vision promises to be more accurate and
compelling than those which previously have shaped human
consciousness.  This emerging vision encompasses the whole of
reality:  the nature and means to knowledge (epistemology),
the nature and purpose of reality (metaphysics), spirituality
and psychology (metapsychology), the values we should hold and
how we should act towards one and other (ethics), and how we
will enforce our ethics (politics).  Below I describe this
emerging world view which I detail in my book-in-progress
Consciousness and Politics.   
  
EPISTEMOLOGY: (the nature of and means of seeking truth) 
Today science and philosophy suggest that there is no
ultimate, unchanging truth.  Physicists note that merely
observing reality changes it.  Some suggest that "natural
laws" are not static and unchanging but ever-evolving. 
Logicians point out the impossibility of constructing
logically consistent arguments.  It seems neither God nor
natural law has created some objective, knowable reality.  
     Even if there was an objective reality, humans would be
incapable of knowing it objectively.  Humans more "construct"
reality and truth than "discover" them.  Each one of us views
reality through a unique "psychic grid" influenced by
society's teachings and our personal experience.  
     Despite the seeming relativism of truth, humans must
endeavor to discover the most accurate knowledge of the nature
of reality and human purpose.  And we must learn how to best
combine the three means to knowledge--intuition, reason and
empirical methods--to do so.  Moreover, knowing that we create
our own "psychic grids", each of us must learn to create our
own reality instead of just going along with what the social,
economic and political "powers-that-be" call reality.    
METAPHYSICS:  (the nature and purpose of reality)   New
paradigm science describes principles of self-organizing which
operate all through reality--from the sub-atomic, chemical,
astronomical, and planetary levels, up into the cellular,
plant, animal and human levels.  Scientists describe differing
but complimentary dynamic and integrative principles which
resemble the "yang" and "yin" described in Taoist philosophy. 
And they see evidence of an inherent drive to evolve more
organized systems and more fully conscious beings.  
     Many scientists believe these principles of self-
organization and evolution are much like consciousness.  They
notice that dynamic and integrative principles act like
imagination, memory and will.  Moreover, there is evidence of
fields of awareness and memory called "morphogenetic" fields
linking similar entities--atoms, chemicals, species of plants
and animals.  These fields communicate knowledge among, and
aid in the physical and social evolution of, each type or
species.  
     Because these principles are like consciousness, and
because their purpose seems to be greater consciousness, I
personally choose to call the very nature and purpose of
reality "consciousness."  Further, I emphasize that the
concept of consciousness must replace the concept of god.  For
if humans recognize we are the product of our own conscious
evolution, not the creation of a transcendent god, we will
recognize ourselves as the creators of human destiny and
reject authoritarians who claim to speak and act in the name
of some superior being.   
  
  
METAPSYCHOLOGY: (spirituality and psychology)  The concepts of
evolution of consciousness and "yang" dynamic and "yin"
integrative principles can help us understand human
spirituality, psychology and interaction.  Psychologists
describe a "need hierarchy" from more basic needs (safety,
belonging, esteem) to more transcendent needs (self-
actualization, humanistic values, peak experiences).  There
seems to be an inherent drive to "rise up" this hierarchy, to
fulfill higher needs, to evolve to higher consciousness.  When
humans remain stuck on fulfilling lower needs and cannot
fulfill higher needs we become bored, frustrated, obsessive,
and/or addicted (to food, drugs, sex, power, money, etc.). 
The need hierarchy and the yogic concept of chakras describe
essentially the same phenomena.  
     Humans rise up the need hierarchy and develop and balance
our chakras through both individual and social efforts. 
Individually, we need awareness of the importance of need
hierarchies and chakras and training in meditation and self-
actualization techniques.  Socially, we must create culture
(including myths, symbols and rituals) and economic and
political institutions which suggest nonextravagant means of
fulfilling basic needs and provide ample opportunities for
fulfilling higher needs.  
     These efforts will allow humans to give up our
addictions, including our need to dominate others.  (This last
also will require that women develop more of their "yang"
independence energies and men develop their "yin" cooperative
energies.)  They will help us develop not only tolerance for,
but enjoyment and celebration of, individual and group
diversity.  And they will allow us to enjoy a higher level of
communication and community with other humans.  
     Studies in past life regression, near-death experience,
psychic phenomena and morphogenetic field theory suggest that
consciousness does survive when the body dies and
"reincarnates," passing from body to body over many life
times.  Learning and evolution occurs in both the individual
and "collective" consciousness.  
  
ETHICS:  (What values we should pursue, how we should act
towards one and other)  The concepts of evolution of con-
sciousness and "yang" dynamic and "yin" integrative prin-
ciples also suggest a specific ethical system.  It is one
which values first and foremost the evolution of consciousness
and all that advances it. "Yin-yang" principles suggest the
libertarian view that the individual should be free to do as
s/he pleases as long as s/he does not harm others; but they
also recommend non-violence, cooperation and mutual aid.     
     The individual decides whether yin or yang acts are most
appropriate from situation to situation.  However, given the
wide range of human personality types--from more yang to more
yin--it is also inevitable that similar types will congregate
in families and  communities which share their preference for
more yin or more yang personal and political ethics and
lifestyles.  
  
POLITICS:  (How we will enforce our ethics)  The ethical
system above rejects the use of force (except in self-defense)
and would rely instead on education, voluntary compliance and
non-violent coercion like publicity and boycott.  
     Again, we can apply "yin-yang" principles.  Individuals
would be free join in self-governing communities with those
who share similar social, political and economic views. 
However, this "yang" communitarianism would be comple-mented
by "yin" mechanisms of non-violent conflict resolution between
communities and aid and assistance to all suffering and
afflicted humans.  
     Today's great nation states have been created and
maintained by violence.  In a non-violent political system
based on liberty and cooperation they quickly would crumble,
to be replaced by networks of cooperative and non-violent
communities.  
  
