Date: Sat, 12 Jun 1993 15:23:16 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: SPACE/FREE: Governments in Space

Tim May:
> Something should be said this time around about the likelihood that space
> colonies will be *very controlled, very totalitarian*, at least for a long
> while. 

This is a popular notion, but I think it's based on the traditional
vision of space colonies as being very large, centralized endeavors
(from Von Braun's massive "conquest of space" to O'Neill colonies
and SPS).  The people who are actually using space (commercial,
miltary) are developing it in a very different manner: 
highly automated networks or "constellations".  As long as computers
are getting smarter faster than people, this trend will accelerate.
As a result, space is likely to be colonized in a manner vastly
different from Von Braun conquests or O'Neill colonies.

The ratio of materials per person (in accessible but crude 
forms such as unoxidized metals, volatiles, etc.) and 
energy/person will be many orders of magnitude higher than on Earth.
Volatiles and metal regoliths will be transported to vast
automated industrial parks.  Trillions of cubic km, trillions of
tons of nickel-rich iron, trillions of tons of water, organics,
etc., millions of km^2 of metallized thin film balloons and mirrors.
Designs will be beamed up from Earth to 3D printers, mills, 
kilometer-scale fabs, self-replicating biotech vats, assemblers, etc. 
which will then construct huge industries to export a wide variety of 
heretofore expensive products to Earth.

Alongside these industries, playing a secondary role will appear
small astronaut outposts.  As launch costs go down and the native
industrial economy matures, it becomes cheap to build vast space 
mansions, eg the foamed-asteroid method.   Each family may live
in a km^3-sized palace, itself inside a foamed-asteroid village kilometers
across.   There may be thousands of such little villages, and
thousands more large industrial parks, scattered across the Belt.  
Space settlers will piggyback on a vastly larger robotic industrial 
economy. 

The military may also play a large role, building automated bases
across the asteroid belt to construct Earth-bombarding javelin
constellations.

We also have to look at general economic trends.
As manufacturing becomes more efficient (eg from early nanotech)
it will dwindle as a share of the worldwide economy, perhaps even
to the proportion that agriculture has today.  Medicine, communications
and other services will come to dominate the economy. 

Side Prediction: Despite all the current ranting & raving about
health care costs being 12% of U.S. GNP, by 2030 medical-related costs
will be 25% of U.S. GNP, with much of that $2-3 trillion going
towards cryonics, smart drugs and other kinds of enhancement.

In the long run it will be a tiny proportion of the wealthy
and upper-middle class of Earth, a million people willing
to pay $1 million ($93) for several thousand acres each of
biosphere out in the Belt, that will make space settlement
really take off.  They may be escaping religious persecution,
political tyranny, etc.  Most of them will pay off the launch
ticket and mortgage with information-work that can be done with 
30-minute net delays to Earth (publishing, design, & the like).  
No government will be able to stop the flow of information
to and from space; nor the flow of high-value goods down
from space to the Earth's surface, past all Coast Gaurds,
blockades, DEA agents, etc.   It's possible that
smuggling of information through space (eg via comsats
over the Iron Curtain) has already played a role in bringing
down tyrannical governments.

Space settlers will be highly dependent on Earth for information 
(machine designs, etc.) but not for materials or physical equipment.  
(If they were so dependent, we couldn't afford to send them in the first
place).

There's plenty of room in this scenario for lone frontiersmen,
libertarian smuggling outposts, cryptoTAZ's, and the like.

