Date: Sat, 12 Jun 93 22:25:38 -0400
From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: DIET: Veggies, threat or menace? :-)
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute freely

 anton@hydra.unm.edu (Stanton McCandlish) writes:
>Considering that plants DO have all sorts of alkaloids in them designed
>to get rid of pests, I find the idea rather disturbing...Is my parsley
>mildly toxic?  Am I being slowly poisoned by my salads?  Sure, that's
>overreatcing, but the core idea could have some validity.  Perhaps plants
>aren't so perfectly healthy.  Anyone seen any studies on this?  Or heard
>of attempts to engineer toxinless plants?

  It is certainly true that any plant with no chemical or physical defenses
has been eaten into extinction. This is a real problem, and if it weren't
for other factors that make vegetables good for my health, I would have
given up many vegetables a while ago.
  Things to remember:
  Ripe fruits are healthy to animals whose body chemistry is sufficiently
close to those which normally disperse the seeds in those fruits.
  Most predators are small (typically insects), and more specialized than
humans, so they normally get a much larger helping of a single plant in 
a given meal (per unit of body weight). Chemicals which are fatal if
consumed in large quantities can accidentally have desirable effects in
small quantities (many drugs derived from plants). Plants don't have a
strong incentive to produce chemicals which kill in small bites, or which
take very long to produce adverse symptoms.

  There is probably a nontrivial correlation between bad taste and poisons
(plants benefit from advertizing their defenses), so consumers provide
some incentive to produce plants with lower toxin levels. However, there
is a countervailing incentive produced by increased crop losses from
defenseless plants.
