From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: MOVIE REVIEW:  _Jurassic_Park_

I sent a short review to Tim May, who suggested I post some comments here.

The dinosaur specials are wonderful -- I was not aware of them as specials,
I just sat there wondering what the animals were going to do next.  The
reptiles indeed are generally much more interesting characters, and much
less artificial, than most of the humans -- but after all, most of the
latter have only (*ahem*) bit parts.

The studio secrecy about most of the scenes was justified by their impact
on the unprepared viewer.  The first face-to-face unobstructed view of a
dinosaur is as mind-blowing as a Saturn launch, and as humbling; I almost
cried.

The movie necessarily watered down much of the math and science described
(in an already watered-down form) in the book, but actually did explain a
modest amount about DNA, and used diverse chaos-theory buzzwords in reasonable
context.  Computers and their use were well-integrated.  There was one
workstation and associated desktop that looked embarrassingly like mine --
you will know it when you see it.  And there were lots of in-group
computer jokes.

And to provide additional extropian content to this posting I will mention
the mild spoiler that one of the first persons who is (as Calvin would put
it) messily devoured is a lawyer.

